  "updated": "2026-07-28T06:42:33.322113+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level trough will dig over the Northeast this morning. This \ntrough will become cutoff late today with a stacked low developing \nover the region. A strong LLJ will develop south of the low and \nswing over to the east side of it. Once on the eastern side, it will \nenhance moisture advection as moisture gets pulled from the Atlantic.\nWith the LLJ over the Poconos and Catskills for a period of time\ncombined with terrain effects, enhanced rainfall amounts are\nexpected over those regions, but other areas like the Wyoming \nValley may see higher rainfall amounts as well with this strong \nflow over that region early today.\n\nThis system will be slow to move eastward while it remains present \nnear the area heading into the overnight hours. A dry slot will\nlikely push in and bring a period of rain-free conditions for\nmany. However, moisture will wrap around the low and additional \nshowers will develop west of the dry slot, leading to showers \ncontinuing throughout Wednesday. Model guidance have a secondary\nlow developing closer to the coast. This low will also be slow \nmoving, so scattered showers are expected to linger through \nThursday.\n\nForecasted rainfall totals will range from 1 to 3 inches across the \nregion. As mentioned, the strong LLJ and terrain effect will likely \nlead to an area of higher rainfall amounts, where locally totals \nwill push into the 3 to 5 inch range in the Catskills and Poconos. \nSome of the 00z guidance is concerning given that localized amounts \nof 6+ inches are shown, and not just in the regions highlighted in \nthe previous statement. Guidance have also been fairly consistent \nwith enhanced totals in the Finger Lakes region after the wraparound \nshowers on Wednesday. In addition, some instability within the warm \nsector will support isolated to scattered thunderstorms. Storms will \nalso have the potential for moderate to heavy rainfall at times. \nShear will be slightly more favorable for isolated stronger storms \nin the Wyoming Valley today. Despite this system being on our \ndoorstep, there is uncertainty on exactly where the heaviest rain \nwill fall as that will depend on where this system sets up.\n\nWith all of the uncertainty in mind, one could make the argument to \nexpanded the watch to all counties in the region, though not all \nwould see hydro issues. To stay in collaboration with neighbors and \ncover most counties covered by the WPC's Slight Risk, the Flood\nWatch now covers most of Northeast PA and parts of Central NY \nalong and east of I-81. The watch could be expanded more if \nconfidence increases on where heavier rain will fall.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThis system will drift up the coast into New England by Thursday, \nthrough wraparound moisture on the back side of the system will \ncontinue to support light showers over the region. These showers \nwill continue through Friday, though a ridge will begin to push in \nfrom the west with drier air. Shower activity should come to an end \nFriday night with a dry start to the weekend expected.\n\nOver the weekend, a trough takes shape over the Northeast once again \nas a low pressure system develops in the Southwest. The flow around \nthis low will tap into the Gulf and Atlantic, potentially advecting \nmoisture northward into the region. There is uncertainty though if \nsurface high pressure keeps conditions dry through Sunday or if \nshowers develop with the trough digging in. Chances for showers may \nbe better early next week as a cold front drops in from the\nnorth.",
